To calculate the population of the city in 15 years given an initial population of 800,000 people growing at 1% per year, you can use the formula for compound interest:

\[ \text{Future Population} = \text{Current Population} \times (1 + \text{growth rate})^{\text{number of years}} \]

Given:
- Current Population = 800,000
- Growth Rate = 1% or 0.01
- Number of Years = 15

\[ \text{Future Population} = 800,000 \times (1 + 0.01)^{15} \]

\[ \text{Future Population} = 800,000 \times (1.01)^{15} \]

\[ \text{Future Population} \approx 800,000 \times 1.161 alfa \]

\[ \text{Future Population} \approx 926,800 \]

So, the population of the city in 15 years will be approximately 926,800 people. 

